-- title:
--   Theorem 3 (companion theorem; not used in the proof of the goal) — Two-LP consistency
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a finite capacitated network and let $\lambda_i\ge0$, $\lambda_i+\beta_i\ge0$ and $\pi_j\ge0$ be the control, treatment and supply rates. Fix $0<\rho<1$. Retain the source standing assumptions: blind allocation, unique optimal primal and dual solutions at the fluid experiment state, and coordinatewise sign consistency of $\beta$. On a common probability space take, at every positive integer density $\tau=n+1$, the source independent Poisson experiment counts. Then the Two-LP estimators are integrable, and
--   $$
--   \widehat\Delta^{n+1}_{2LP}\longrightarrow\Delta\quad\text{almost surely},\qquad
--   \mathbb E[\widehat\Delta^{n+1}_{2LP}]\longrightarrow\Delta,
--   \quad\Delta=\Phi(\lambda+\beta,\pi)-\Phi(\lambda,\pi).
--   $$
--   The first conclusion states consistency of the observed estimator; the second states its asymptotic unbiasedness, as described immediately after Theorem 3. Both use the actual count laws rather than true rates substituted into the estimator.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Positive integer densities are indexed by $n+1$; no zero-density division occurs. The allocation data retain Assumption 1 even though the estimator depends only on counts. No independence across densities or desired limit is assumed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: finite source types and path sums → zero-based Fin types, finite arc types and finite-support path flows; reason: the same finite network and all of its supply-to-demand paths, including intermediate nodes
--   - reading: an unspecified fluid limit and surrounding asymptotic unbiasedness → almost-sure convergence along positive integer densities and convergence of expectations, with integrability; reason: Theorem 1 and the Poisson-process setting used by the p. 43 proof identify this reading; finite capacities uniformly bound the scaled estimator
--   - generalization: counts generated by the source Poisson processes across densities → any common-space coupling with the prescribed independent-coordinate marginals at each density; reason: summable exponential Poisson deviations give almost-sure convergence for every such coupling, including the source coupling
-- source:
--   Bright, Delarue and Lobel, Reducing Marketplace Interference Bias Via Shadow Prices, arXiv:2205.02274v4, p. 17, Theorem 3 and asymptotic-unbiasedness discussion; p. 43, proof

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_MarketInterference_FluidBias_Supplement_RandomAllocation

namespace MarketInterference.FluidBias.Supplement

open MeasureTheory Filter

theorem theorem_3 {nd ns : ℕ} {I E Ω : Type} [Fintype I] [Fintype E]
    [DecidableEq I] [DecidableEq E] [MeasurableSpace Ω]
    (N : Network nd ns I E) (lam beta : Fin nd → ℝ) (pi : Fin ns → ℝ) (ρ : ℝ)
    (hlam : ∀ i, 0 ≤ lam i) (htreat : ∀ i, 0 ≤ lam i + beta i) (hpi : ∀ j, 0 ≤ pi j)
    (hρ : 0 < ρ) (hρ1 : ρ < 1)
    (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] (X : ℕ → Ω → Counts nd ns)
    (hlaw : ∀ n, HasExperimentLaw μ (X n) lam beta pi ρ (n + 1 : ℕ))
    (A : ∀ n, BlindAllocation N μ (X n) (n + 1 : ℕ))
    (hprimal : ∃! x, IsOptimal N 1 (lam + ρ • beta) pi x)
    (hdual : ∃! q, IsOptimalDual N 1 (lam + ρ • beta) pi q)
    (hsign : (∀ i, 0 ≤ beta i) ∨ (∀ i, beta i ≤ 0)) :
    (∀ n, Integrable (fun ω => twoLP N ρ (n + 1 : ℕ) (X n ω)) μ) ∧
    (∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, Tendsto (fun n => twoLP N ρ (n + 1 : ℕ) (X n ω)) atTop
      (nhds (Delta N lam beta pi))) ∧
    Tendsto (fun n => ∫ ω, twoLP N ρ (n + 1 : ℕ) (X n ω) ∂μ) atTop
      (nhds (Delta N lam beta pi)) := by sorry

end MarketInterference.FluidBias.Supplement
